 {:db/id "0ad61779-481f-5a72-a1fc-d5ace5fa7378"
  :question/id #uuid "0ad61779-481f-5a72-a1fc-d5ace5fa7378"
  :question/problem "Source question: Assignment item (2); Kuttler 2017A, Exercise 5.2.4, printed p. 278 (PDF p. 290). The handout recommends Procedure 5.10 and Example 5.11.\n\nSuppose $T$ is a linear transformation such that\n$$T\\!\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\1\\\\-8\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\3\\\\1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\nT\\!\\begin{pmatrix}-1\\\\0\\\\6\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}2\\\\4\\\\1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\nT\\!\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\-1\\\\3\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}6\\\\1\\\\-1\\end{pmatrix}.$$\nFind the matrix $A$ of $T$, that is, $T(\\mathbf x)=A\\mathbf x$. Show supporting work.\n\n$A=$ {{matrix}}."
  :question/answer-fields ["6eefb71d-cda8-56ee-98d2-bb30a6a8fac8" "210bdfeb-e6e5-5c23-86d5-d7ccc9190c3e"]
  :question/worked-solution "The given inputs are not the standard basis: their images are the columns of $AP$, not directly the columns of $A$. Solve $AP=Q$ by right-multiplying by $P^{-1}$, or solve the three corresponding systems for the rows of $A$."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
