 {:db/id "46bf94b4-458b-5c3e-804b-042b8a4fd877"
  :question/id #uuid "46bf94b4-458b-5c3e-804b-042b8a4fd877"
  :question/problem "Source question: Assignment item (4); Kuttler 2017A, Exercise 5.2.9(a) only, printed p. 280 (PDF p. 292).\n\nConsider $T:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^2$ defined by\n$$T\\!\\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\y\\\\z\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}x+2y+3z+1\\\\2y-3x+z\\end{pmatrix}.$$\nExplain why $T$ is not linear."
  :question/answer-fields ["0c278b5b-4ec2-5e77-89be-a83c2a7b3a5e"]
  :question/worked-solution "Test a necessary linearity property. The constant $+1$ makes the zero input produce a nonzero output. A counterexample disproves linearity; checking a few successful inputs would not prove it."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
