 {:db/id "01775553-2e48-5875-9150-22cb6950bb30"
  :answer/id #uuid "01775553-2e48-5875-9150-22cb6950bb30"
  :answer/type :answer.type/text
  :answer/value "Let $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^n$ be arbitrary. Since the $\\mathbf v_i$ form a basis, there are scalars $c_1,\\ldots,c_n$ with $\\mathbf x=\\sum_{i=1}^n c_i\\mathbf v_i$. By linearity and the assumed equality on basis vectors,\n$$S(\\mathbf x)=S\\!\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^n c_i\\mathbf v_i\\right)=\\sum_{i=1}^n c_iS(\\mathbf v_i)=\\sum_{i=1}^n c_iT(\\mathbf v_i)=T\\!\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^n c_i\\mathbf v_i\\right)=T(\\mathbf x).$$\nThus $S$ and $T$ agree on every vector in their common domain, so $S=T$. Spanning supplies the expansion; the proof does not require computing its coefficients."
  :db/ensure :answer/validate}
